# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether the API runs on a node; applying it is symlink
# manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Starting it is the
# node's post-transaction hook's job — and that hook will NOT start this one until the signing key
# below is set, because a blank key means a per-process key and everyone is signed out on every
# restart.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled this unit keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-api.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-api is enabled and waiting on one value:

    Api__SigningKey  in  /etc/kgsm-api/kgsm-api.env      openssl rand -base64 48

  Set it, then start the service. It must not change between restarts — a rotated key invalidates
  every session and refresh token that was issued under the old one.

    systemctl start kgsm-api.service

  The first successful start creates the first administrator and prints its password once:

    /opt/kgsm-api/kgsm-api user bootstrap

  If people here sign in through Discord or another provider, that application goes in
  /etc/kgsm/kgsm-auth.env, which every surface on the host reads. A host whose people sign in with a
  KGSM password needs nothing there.

  Install kgsm-web for the Control Panel UI. Without it the API serves its endpoints and no page,
  which is a supported way to run a node.

  Run kgsm-node-status at any time for what this host runs and what is still outstanding.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-api/kgsm-api.env, merge it — new settings are declared
  there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
